          "narrative": "The revolutionary temper of Bhagat Singh was neither an accident of youth nor an imported ideology; it was the biological and cultural inheritance of generations rooted in the soil of Khatkar Kalan. Situated in the Doaba region of Punjab, Khatkar Kalan was a fortified ancestral village whose Sandhu Jat clan possessed a fierce tradition of resistance dating back to the Anglo-Sikh wars of the 1840s.\n\nBhagat Singh’s grandfather, Sardar Arjun Singh, was a man of profound moral conviction—an early Arya Samaj reformer who blended Vedic reformism with open disdain for feudal subservience. His three sons—Kishen Singh (Bhagat Singh's father), Ajit Singh, and Swaran Singh—grew to maturity in an atmosphere charged with agrarian crisis and colonial exploitation. Under British colonial irrigation schemes, the peasantry of the Chenab and Bari Doab canal colonies were subjected to crushing water rates, draconian colonization bills, and arbitrary tenancy laws that reduced proud freeholders to serfs on their own lands.\n\nIn response, the family home became an underground clearinghouse for nationalist literature and anti-colonial organizing. When Bhagat Singh was born on September 28, 1907, in the village of Banga (Lyallpur district, now in Pakistan), his birth coincided with the release of his father Kishen Singh from Central Jail, Lahore, and his uncle Swaran Singh from Bareilly jail, while his uncle Sardar Ajit Singh was returning from exile in Mandalay. Celebrating this auspicious convergence, his grandmother declared the newborn child 'Bhaganwala'—the child of good fortune—which was shortened to Bhagat.",
