          "narrative": "Between February 17 and March 5, 1931, Mahatma Gandhi and Viceroy Lord Irwin held historic negotiations that culminated in the Gandhi-Irwin Pact. With the entire nation pleading for the lives of Bhagat Singh, Sukhdev, and Rajguru, public pressure was overwhelming for Gandhi to make the commutation of their death sentences an absolute condition for signing the Pact.\n\nDid Gandhi do everything in his power to save them? A rigorous, forensic comparison of Lord Irwin's confidential daily minutes, Gandhi's public statements, and the memoirs of Home Secretary H.W. Emerson reveals a tragic, uncomfortable truth. Gandhi did raise the question with Irwin on multiple occasions, asking for a suspension or postponement of the execution. However, Gandhi explicitly told Irwin that he was *not* making commutation a breaking condition of the Pact: 'If the boys are to be hanged, it would be better to hang them before the Karachi Congress than after.'\n\nIrwin noted in his secret dispatch to Secretary of State Wedgwood Benn:\n'Mr. Gandhi said that he would not plead for their lives, for he could not defend violence. He only asked whether their execution might be postponed until after the Congress session. I told him that postponement was impossible and would only raise false hopes.' Gandhi's philosophical commitment to absolute non-violence prevented him from exerting the political leverage that could have forced the British to commute the sentences to life imprisonment.",
