          "narrative": "On March 22, Bhagat Singh wrote his final letter to his fellow prisoners of the Second Lahore Conspiracy Case: 'The desire to live is natural in man. I also have it. But I can live only on one condition—that I should not be imprisoned in a cage. My name has become the symbol of the Indian revolution... If I die with courage, facing the scaffold with a smile, Indian mothers will wish their children to become Bhagat Singh.'\n\nOn the afternoon of March 23, around 4:00 PM, his legal counsel Pran Nath Mehta was granted a final twenty-minute interview. When Mehta entered cell No. 14, he found Bhagat Singh pacing with calm energy, reading a newly delivered book: Clara Zetkin's *Reminiscences of Lenin*. When Mehta asked if he had any message for the country, Bhagat Singh replied with two crisp sentences: 'Down with Imperialism!' and 'Long Live Revolution!'\n\nWhen jail wardens approached his cell two hours earlier than customary, announcing that the time had come, Bhagat Singh did not drop the book. He turned a page, looked up at the jailer, and smiled:\n\n'Wait! Do you not see that one revolutionary is meeting another revolutionary?'\n\nHe finished the chapter, closed the book, tossed it toward the ceiling, and stood up: 'Let us go.'",
