          "date": "March 23, 1931, 7:00 PM – Dawn, March 24",
          "narrative": "In a blatant, desperate violation of the Punjab Jail Manual—which mandated that executions take place at 6:00 AM at dawn—the British authorities brought forward the execution by eleven hours to **7:00 PM on the evening of March 23, 1931**. They feared that if the hangings occurred the next morning, hundreds of thousands of people would storm the jail.\n\nArm in arm, Bhagat Singh (age 23), Sukhdev Thapar (age 23), and Shivaram Rajguru (age 22) walked from their cells toward the scaffold. Their voices rose together in the quiet dusk, singing their favorite verse:\n\n'Dil se niklegi na mar kar bhi vatan ki ulfat, / Meri mitti se bhi khushbu-e-vatan aayegi.'\n(Even after death, love for the motherland will not leave my heart; from my very dust, the fragrance of my country will arise.)\n\nAs they mounted the wooden platform, they kissed the hangman's noose. At exactly 7:33 PM, the trapdoors fell. Death was instantaneous.\n\nWhat followed was an act of imperial cowardice that horrified civilized opinion worldwide. Refusing to hand the bodies over to their weeping families waiting outside the main gate, the prison authorities smuggled the three corpses out through a breach knocked into the rear wall of the jail. Loaded onto military trucks, the bodies were driven under blackout to the desolate banks of the Sutlej river at Ganda Singh Wala near Ferozepur. There, under the dark sky, British officers and Sikh and Hindu jail staff poured kerosene over the bodies and lit a crude bonfire. Before the bodies were fully cremated, seeing torches of approaching villagers who had grown suspicious of the smoke, the British soldiers kicked the half-burned remains into the freezing waters of the Sutlej and fled in their trucks.\n\nThe next morning, the riverbank was discovered. Grief and fury swept the subcontinent. When Mahatma Gandhi arrived at the Karachi railway station for the Congress session on March 25, thousands of angry young men greeted him with black flowers and shouts of 'Where is Bhagat Singh?'. Though his physical body was consumed by flame and river, Bhagat Singh had achieved what imperial executioners could never extinguish: he had become immortal.",
